{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-f7e73b21-bd96-4fcd-8044-8a185e72785d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0b47b620ff78f74","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-zaz9tdba/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n\nOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n\n## Triage Outcome\n\nStep 0 was evaluated first. The strategy is S-sized (1–2 sprints), affects a single\ncomponent (rhods-operator), and a single team performs all code work (ODH Operator\nteam), with highly mechanical, pattern-following conversion work. However, the\n**multiple distinct work streams** escape condition fires: the scope spans (a) code\nimplementation in the manager binary, (b) code implementation in the architecturally\ndistinct cloudmanager binary (separate binary, separate controllers, deployed on\nnon-OpenShift Kubernetes: AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), and (c) substantive\ndocumentation authoring — a new contribution guideline specifying the convention and\nits publication as a specification for external operator adoption (P1 HLR). This is\nnew content authoring, not an incidental documentation touch-up. Below-threshold does\nnot apply; the full decomposition (Steps 1–8) was executed.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section was parsed first and takes precedence. The\nstrategy body already incorporates the SME corrections, so no divergence remained to\noverride: the corrected controller architecture (5 tiers: DSC, 8 component\ncontrollers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI, 5 service controllers),\nthe exact action-file breakdown (15 total: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3\nmodule), the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard\nkeys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`), the camelCase field\nnaming convention, and cloudmanager's use of the shared reconciler (already gets\n`name`/`namespace`). The wrong-premise open question about \"~16 component action\nfiles\" is answered (8/8 components have exactly one) and was not decomposed.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\n- The strategy names one affected component: **rhods-operator** (manager +\n  cloudmanager binaries). This name is not verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`;\n  it is mapped to the canonical component **`OpenShift AI Productization`** — the\n  RHOAI platform operator maintained by the ODH Operator team in the downstream\n  `red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator` repository. All three epics use this single\n  canonical component. Logged here as a non-obvious mapping.\n- External operators (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator, owned by the Cluster\n  Observability Operator team) are **not** components of this decomposition: per\n  platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external\n  operator dependencies. The strategy only publishes the convention for voluntary\n  adoption, which is a deliverable of the documentation epic. No COO-team epic is\n  generated and no passive-component validation epic is needed (nothing in RHOAI\n  validates their behavior).\n\n## Investigation Scan\n\nAll unknowns were collected systematically (open questions table, risks, assumptions,\npending reviews) and each evaluated against the decision rule — *does the answer\nchange which downstream epics exist or what they do?*\n\n| Unknown | Source | Structural? | Handling |\n|---------|--------|-------------|----------|\n| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., attempt count, error category)? | Open question (PM / Observability) | No — the required field set is fixed by the HLRs; extra fields would be an additive follow-up on the same call sites, not different epics | Open question recorded on the documentation epic; the guideline records the decision |\n| Which existing log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns that will change? | Open question (SRE / Support) + risk | No — identifying them *is* the log-consumer regression validation step; work proceeds the same way, failure = fix-and-retry | Acceptance criterion + risk on both implementation epics |\n| Should cloudmanager ship in the same release as manager, or a subsequent one? | Open question (PM / ODH Operator) | No — a scheduling decision; the binary boundary already makes the work independently schedulable, and no DAG edge exists between the binaries | Health warning; either scheduling is supported without restructuring |\n| What is the actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity (SME says 75%)? | Open question + assumption | No — the audit is the first work item of the conversion epics; the method (grep/static analysis) is fixed and the answer changes volume, not epic structure or approach | Acceptance criterion (audit checklist) on both implementation epics |\n| Strategy prerequisites (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.) pending human review | Prerequisites & Process Gates | No — strategy-level process gates preceding all implementation work equally | Not decomposed into epics; reflected in the `human_process_gates` signal on each epic |\n\n**No Investigation epics.** No conditional branches or phased decomposition are\nneeded.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\nComponent for all three epics: `OpenShift AI Productization` (see Component Mapping).\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E001<br/>manager binary log conversion (P0)\"] --> E003\n    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E002<br/>cloudmanager binary log conversion (P0)\"] --> E003\n    E003[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E003<br/>convention documentation (P1)\"]\n```\n\nE001 and E002 run in parallel (no edge between them).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` epic is blocked by every Implementation epic: the guideline documents what was built (before/after examples, DSCI migration guide) and must be technically reviewed against the implemented conversions |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same — the published convention covers both binaries |\n| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repository, different areas: the manager binary (OpenShift) and the cloudmanager binary (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave) share no artifacts — the field convention is a shared *standard*, not a build-against artifact. Potential merge conflicts are coordination risk, not a dependency |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nHLRs are numbered in strategy order (HLR-1 … HLR-8).\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 — embedded resource identity converted to structured `name`/`namespace` in all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs | P0 | E001 (manager binary), E002 (cloudmanager) | Surface split by binary |\n| HLR-2 — DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-3 — service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-4 — all 15 action file error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` across 4 tiers | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (cross-controller queryability) | P1 | E001 **(bundled)** | Bundled: the DSC and component-controller `resourceKind` addition lands on the *same `log.Error` call sites* E001 already restructures for HLR-1 in the same files. A separate P1 epic would force a second pass over every converted call site and duplicate the audit checklist and lint wiring — not separately schedulable. E001 is P0 regardless via its P0 HLRs |\n| HLR-8 — convention documented as contribution guideline and published for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | |\n\nAll eight HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n**Priority derivation:** E001 and E002 are P0 by their P0 HLRs. E003 is P1 via the\n`docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1),\nmatching HLR-8's P1; its edges do not push priority onto the implementations.\n\n**Enforcement deliverable placement:** the CI lint/static-analysis rule the strategy\nrequires is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable. It is *not* a standalone epic —\nit is divided per surface: the manager-binary conversion epic owns the lint check for\nmanager binary sources, and the cloudmanager conversion epic owns the mirror item for\ncloudmanager sources (each wired into CI for its own binary).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Epic kept above the ~2-week guideline (E001).** Effort estimate ~1.25–1.5 sprints\n  (audit ~0.5 + conversion ~0.75–1, per the strategy's estimate). Kept as one epic\n  because it is a single logical sweep over one binary's reconciliation error-log\n  surface: the audit checklist, field convention, unit-test pattern, and CI lint\n  wiring are shared across all five controller tiers, and a per-tier split would\n  duplicate that scaffolding and fragment one convention change across artificial\n  boundaries. **Conditional re-split trigger:** re-split by controller tier (e.g.,\n  component controllers + action files / module controller / service controllers /\n  DSC + DSCI) if the completed call-site audit finds materially more\n  embedded-identity sites than the ~75% estimate implies (roughly more than double\n  the expected site count), or if a single release train cannot absorb the review\n  load.\n- **Cloudmanager release timing (open question, PM-owned).** Whether cloudmanager\n  lands in the same release as the manager binary is a scheduling decision, not a\n  structural one; the decomposition keeps the two binaries independent (no DAG edge),\n  so either scheduling is supported without restructuring.\n- **Log consumer regression risk.** Message strings change and DSCI key names migrate;\n  existing text-based alert rules (Loki, CloudWatch) may break. Captured as\n  acceptance criteria and risks on both implementation epics (SRE/Support\n  coordination, dev-cluster ingestion validation before merge). The single-release\n  DSCI transition and before/after query examples are documented in the docs epic.\n- **Strategy ACs are marked \"requires PM/Engineering validation.\"** Epic acceptance\n  criteria were derived from the proposed ACs as written; if PM/Engineering validation\n  changes them, the epic ACs should be revisited.\n- No priority inversions detected: P0/P1 assignment follows the HLR priorities; the\n  only P1 implementation HLR (HLR-7) is bundled into the P0 conversion pass on shared\n  call sites (see traceability matrix).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. The only external dependency — Cluster Observability Operator team\nadoption of the convention — is explicitly non-gating per platform policy (overlay\n0008: RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies) and\nper the RFE approval comment: RHOAI's deliverable is publishing the convention, with\nadoption at COO's discretion. No epic is blocked on external action, so there is no\nTier 1/Tier 2 split and no upstream Implementation epic. The controller-runtime logr\ndependency is internal and confirmed to exist (architecture context:\n`github.com/go-logr/logr` v1.4.3 in `red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator`).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":58094,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8972947215b9907","agent_id":"a101f61d943c34416","session_id":"f64427cd-cf3e-4bee-9716-c63ae53a7129","uuid":"b73a942a-ef6c-468e-a040-3141756321c4","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:37:45.661Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010 into epics"}
