{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-985f454e-7d2c-4f56-8278-1ce2d476e737","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b3cf7df091b9e6d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-zaz9tdba/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context\n\n## Description\n\nApplies the same structured-log standardization to the rhods-operator cloudmanager\nbinary, which ships with the operator and handles multi-cloud infrastructure\ndependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its three\nreconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\nCoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation\nusing the shared reconciler pattern, so they already receive `name` and `namespace`\nfrom controller-runtime's request logger. This epic converts message-embedded\nresource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs, adds the\n`resourceKind` field, and migrates any non-standard key names to the camelCase\nconvention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit every `log.Error()` call site in reconciliation error paths across the three\n  cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) using grep/static analysis, with a coverage checklist\n  marking each site converted or not-applicable.\n- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string,\n  extract the identity fields and convert them to structured key-value pairs\n  (`\"name\"`, `\"namespace\"`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager controller\n  reconciliation error logs (the kind is not implied by the shared reconciler\n  pattern, so this is a functional requirement here).\n- Migrate any non-standard structured key names to the camelCase convention, if\n  present in these controllers.\n- Handle cluster-scoped infrastructure resources: emit `name` and `resourceKind`,\n  with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — never a spurious value.\n- Add unit tests that parse test logger output and assert `name`, `namespace`, and\n  `resourceKind` presence and correctness for the cloudmanager controllers.\n- Add the lint/static-analysis check for cloudmanager sources, wired into CI for the\n  cloudmanager binary, flagging `log.Error` calls embedding name/namespace in message\n  text or missing the required structured fields.\n- Backwards-compatibility validation: before/after log output comparison for a\n  sample reconciliation error, confirming the production log consumers on the\n  affected clouds (e.g., CloudWatch) ingest both formats without configuration\n  changes.\n- Coordinate with SRE/Support to identify text-based alert rules that rely on the\n  changed message-string patterns and document the required updates.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. A complete audit checklist of every `log.Error()` reconciliation-error call site\n   in the cloudmanager controllers exists (produced by grep/static analysis), with\n   each site marked converted or not-applicable.\n2. Given a cloudmanager controller reconciliation error log call that previously\n   embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, the updated call uses\n   structured key-value pairs (`\"name\"`, `\"namespace\"`) — measured by the CI lint\n   rule reporting zero remaining `log.Error` calls with identity embedded in message\n   text in cloudmanager sources.\n3. Unit tests parsing test logger output assert that cloudmanager controller\n   (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine)\n   reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` with\n   correct values.\n4. Unit tests confirm cluster-scoped infrastructure resources emit `name` and\n   `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or empty — no spurious namespace value.\n5. No non-standard structured key names remain in cloudmanager controller error logs\n   — measured by unit test asserting camelCase field names and a codebase grep.\n6. The lint/static-analysis check is wired into CI for the cloudmanager binary and\n   flags `log.Error` calls that embed name/namespace in message text or omit required\n   structured fields.\n7. A before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error shows the\n   structured fields present, and the production log consumers on the affected clouds\n   ingest both formats without configuration changes — verified before merge.\n8. Text-based alert rules affected by the changed message strings are identified\n   together with SRE/Support and an update plan is documented before merge.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name\n  or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs —\n  cloudmanager binary scope of this requirement.\n- HLR-5 (P0): all cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine,\n  AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include\n  `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n\n## Risks\n\n- **Release timing:** whether the cloudmanager conversion ships in the same release\n  as the manager binary conversion or a subsequent one is an open product decision;\n  this work is independently schedulable and narrower in impact (cloudmanager is only\n  deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes).\n- **Log consumer regression:** cloud log consumers (e.g., CloudWatch) or text-based\n  alert rules may fail on the new format — mitigated by the before/after ingestion\n  validation before merge (AC 7) and SRE/Support coordination (AC 8).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":67905,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8972947215b9907","agent_id":"a101f61d943c34416","session_id":"f64427cd-cf3e-4bee-9716-c63ae53a7129","uuid":"63a91fdb-56ec-4e55-8148-effb0fbd2738","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:38:25.345Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010 into epics"}
