{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94a0f9c0d3ebc67a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource\n4\t  context\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 7\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tApplies the same structured-log standardization to the rhods-operator cloudmanager\n35\tbinary, which ships with the operator and handles multi-cloud infrastructure\n36\tdependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its three\n37\treconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n38\tCoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation\n39\tusing the shared reconciler pattern, so they already receive `name` and `namespace`\n40\tfrom controller-runtime's request logger. This epic converts message-embedded\n41\tresource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs, adds the\n42\t`resourceKind` field, and migrates any non-standard key names to the camelCase\n43\tconvention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`).\n44\t\n45\t## Scope\n46\t\n47\t- Audit every `log.Error()` call site in reconciliation error paths across the three\n48\t  cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n49\t  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) using grep/static analysis, with a coverage checklist\n50\t  marking each site converted or not-applicable.\n51\t- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string,\n52\t  extract the identity fields and convert them to structured key-value pairs\n53\t  (`\"name\"`, `\"namespace\"`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n54\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager controller\n55\t  reconciliation error logs (the kind is not implied by the shared reconciler\n56\t  pattern, so this is a functional requirement here).\n57\t- Migrate any non-standard structured key names to the camelCase convention, if\n58\t  present in these controllers.\n59\t- Handle cluster-scoped infrastructure resources: emit `name` and `resourceKind`,\n60\t  with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — never a spurious value.\n61\t- Add unit tests that parse test logger output and assert `name`, `namespace`, and\n62\t  `resourceKind` presence and correctness for the cloudmanager controllers.\n63\t- Add the lint/static-analysis check for cloudmanager sources, wired into CI for the\n64\t  cloudmanager binary, flagging `log.Error` calls embedding name/namespace in message\n65\t  text or missing the required structured fields.\n66\t- Backwards-compatibility validation: before/after log output comparison for a\n67\t  sample reconciliation error, confirming the production log consumers on the\n68\t  affected clouds (e.g., CloudWatch) ingest both formats without configuration\n69\t  changes.\n70\t- Coordinate with SRE/Support to identify text-based alert rules that rely on the\n71\t  changed message-string patterns and document the required updates.\n72\t\n73\t## Acceptance Criteria\n74\t\n75\t1. A complete audit checklist of every `log.Error()` reconciliation-error call site\n76\t   in the cloudmanager controllers exists (produced by grep/static analysis), with\n77\t   each site marked converted or not-applicable.\n78\t2. Given a cloudmanager controller reconciliation error log call that previously\n79\t   embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, the updated call uses\n80\t   structured key-value pairs (`\"name\"`, `\"namespace\"`) — measured by the CI lint\n81\t   rule reporting zero remaining `log.Error` calls with identity embedded in message\n82\t   text in cloudmanager sources.\n83\t3. Unit tests parsing test logger output assert that cloudmanager controller\n84\t   (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine)\n85\t   reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` with\n86\t   correct values.\n87\t4. Unit tests confirm cluster-scoped infrastructure resources emit `name` and\n88\t   `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or empty — no spurious namespace value.\n89\t5. No non-standard structured key names remain in cloudmanager controller error logs\n90\t   — measured by unit test asserting camelCase field names and a codebase grep.\n91\t6. The lint/static-analysis check is wired into CI for the cloudmanager binary and\n92\t   flags `log.Error` calls that embed name/namespace in message text or omit required\n93\t   structured fields.\n94\t7. A before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error shows the\n95\t   structured fields present, and the production log consumers on the affected clouds\n96\t   ingest both formats without configuration changes — verified before merge.\n97\t8. Text-based alert rules affected by the changed message strings are identified\n98\t   together with SRE/Support and an update plan is documented before merge.\n99\t\n100\t## HLR Traceability\n101\t\n102\t- HLR-1 (P0): all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name\n103\t  or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs —\n104\t  cloudmanager binary scope of this requirement.\n105\t- HLR-5 (P0): all cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine,\n106\t  AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include\n107\t  `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n108\t\n109\t## Risks\n110\t\n111\t- **Release timing:** whether the cloudmanager conversion ships in the same release\n112\t  as the manager binary conversion or a subsequent one is an open product decision;\n113\t  this work is independently schedulable and narrower in impact (cloudmanager is only\n114\t  deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes).\n115\t- **Log consumer regression:** cloud log consumers (e.g., CloudWatch) or text-based\n116\t  alert rules may fail on the new format — mitigated by the before/after ingestion\n117\t  validation before merge (AC 7) and SRE/Support coordination (AC 8).\n118\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9255ab7e94130209","agent_id":"a54dfb242773c6fb6","session_id":"f64427cd-cf3e-4bee-9716-c63ae53a7129","uuid":"f010cdeb-2340-4109-89bc-8ce1c24369b9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:40:22.057Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-94a0f9c0d3ebc67a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
