{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e8525147f069d1e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n3\ttitle: Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\tai_signals:\n14\t  change_specificity: 1\n15\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n16\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n17\t  existing_foundation: 0\n18\t  open_questions: 0\n19\t  external_dependency: 0\n20\t  human_process_gates: -1\n21\t  repo_access: 1\n22\t  architecture_claims: 1\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: Medium\n25\tai_implementability_score: 2\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tAuthors the structured log field convention as a contribution guideline for new\n36\tcontroller development in the rhods-operator, and publishes it as a specification for\n37\tvoluntary adoption by external operator teams — the Cluster Observability Operator\n38\tteam's Loki Operator and Tempo Operator — per platform policy (overlay 0008: RHOAI\n39\tdoes not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies). The guideline\n40\tcaptures the three-problem diagnosis behind the standardization (message-embedded\n41\tidentity, non-standard DSCI key names, missing `resourceKind`) and the camelCase field\n42\tconvention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) aligned with controller-runtime\n43\tpractice.\n44\t\n45\t## Scope\n46\t\n47\t- Contribution guideline for new controller development specifying:\n48\t  - the required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`);\n49\t  - the camelCase naming rationale and its alignment with controller-runtime's\n50\t    structured log key conventions;\n51\t  - the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys,\n52\t    missing `resourceKind`);\n53\t  - before/after code examples for converting message-embedded resource identity to\n54\t    structured key-value pairs;\n55\t  - the treatment of cluster-scoped resources (`namespace` omitted or empty).\n56\t- DSCI key-name migration guide for operators: before/after query examples for\n57\t  existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on the retired `Request.Name` /\n58\t  `DSCInitialization` field names, and release-notes documentation so the transition\n59\t  lands in a single release.\n60\t- Publish the convention as a specification for external operator adoption and file\n61\t  tracking issues in the Cluster Observability Operator projects (Loki Operator,\n62\t  Tempo Operator); adoption remains at their discretion per platform policy.\n63\t- Record the PM/Observability team decision on whether the convention should include\n64\t  fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error\n65\t  category) and the associated log-volume trade-off.\n66\t\n67\t## Acceptance Criteria\n68\t\n69\t1. The contribution guideline exists and specifies the required fields (`name`,\n70\t   `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the\n71\t   three-problem diagnosis, with before/after code examples for converting\n72\t   message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs.\n73\t2. The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented manager-binary and\n74\t   cloudmanager conversions, and the review sign-off is recorded.\n75\t3. The DSCI key-name migration is documented with before/after query examples for\n76\t   affected log queries, and the release-notes entry targets a single release.\n77\t4. The convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are\n78\t