{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d56e4f553892ba9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: E001/E002 human_process_gates scored 0 while the strategy lists seven\n10\t    pending human review gates (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation,\n11\t    etc.) that the Investigation Scan says precede all implementation work equally\n12\t    — a strict rubric reading supports -1; E003 external_dependency 0 for filing tracking\n13\t    issues in COO projects is likewise arguable. Both defensible but borderline.\n14\terror: null\n15\t---\n16\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8010\n17\t\n18\t## Review Summary\n19\t\n20\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n21\tRecommendation: accept\n22\t\n23\tContract validator: OK (`skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8010` exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and shared-surface enforcement deliverables all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 3-epic output is expected and not penalized. No branch files exist and none are needed (no conditional branches).\n24\t\n25\t## Criterion Details\n26\t\n27\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n28\t\n29\tAll eight HLRs map to epics via the traceability matrix: HLR-1 through HLR-4 and HLR-6 to E001 (manager binary), HLR-5 to E002 (cloudmanager), HLR-7 bundled into E001, HLR-8 to E003. HLR-1 is correctly split by binary surface (E001 + E002), matching the strategy's manager/cloudmanager scope division.\n30\t\n31\tPriority inheritance: E001 and E002 are P0 by their P0 HLRs; nothing P0 is blocked by lower-priority work. E003 is P1 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1), which matches HLR-8's P1 — correct application of the exception, and its Rule 11 edges correctly do not propagate priority onto it.\n32\t\n33\tPriority collapse check on HLR-7 (P1 bundled into P0 E001): justified, not a deferrable distinct feature — the DSC/component-controller `resourceKind` addition lands on the same `log.Error` call sites E001 already restructures for HLR-1 in the same files; a separate P1 epic would require a second pass over every converted site. The bundling decision is readable directly from the matrix row (`(bundled)` flag with inline rationale), satisfying the bundling-recording check.\n34\t\n35\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n36\t\n37\tEdges: E001 → E003 and E002 → E003 (Rule 11, implementations block docs-authoring — the guideline documents and is technically reviewed against both conversions). Frontmatter `dependencies` match the diagram exactly (E003 lists E001 and E002; E001/E002 have empty lists), and the diagram uses the correct dependency → dependent arrow convention.\n38\t\n39\tE001 ∥ E002 parallelism is correct under Rule 15: same repository, different binaries with no shared artifacts — the field convention is a shared standard specified by the strategy, not a build-against artifact. Each epic owns its own audit checklist, unit tests, and per-binary CI lint wiring, so no hidden data/artifact dependency exists between them. Cross-check of epic content found no consumed artifact lacking an edge.\n40\t\n41\tCritical path length 2 fits the S-size heuristic (1-2). No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable. No Investigation epics, so no `gated_by`/branch-file hazards.\n42\t\n43\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n44\t\n45\tAll three epics share one team (ODH Operator team) and one canonical component (`OpenShift AI Productization`, verified present in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; the non-verbatim `rhods-operator` → canonical mapping is logged in the summary's Component Mapping section).\n46\t\n47\tSize check on E001 (~1.25–1.5 sprints, above the ~2-week guideline): classified as one logical change above the guideline, not a heterogeneous monolith — a single sweep over one binary's reconciliation error-log surface sharing one audit checklist, field convention, test pattern, and lint wiring. All three required Health Warning elements are present (estimate, rationale, and a concrete conditional re-split trigger keyed to audit findings and release-train load), so this is a watch item only.\n48\t\n49\tNo scope duplication between E001 and E002 (disjoint binaries and controllers). No work-product type mixing: code implementation (E001/E002) is separated from content authoring (E003). The CI lint/static-analysis enforcement deliverable is correctly divided per surface into each consuming epic's Scope and Acceptance Criteria (E001 AC 9, E002 AC 6) rather than a standalone epic.\n50\t\n51\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n52\t\n53\tNo Investigation epics — the Investigation Scan table evaluates every unknown (open questions, risks, assumptions, pending gates) against the \"does the answer change epic structure?\" test and correctly finds none structural: the audit-percentage question changes volume, the alert-rule inventory is validation work, and cloudmanager release timing is scheduling. All three epics are Implementation producing concrete artifacts (converted call sites + tests + lint rule; published guideline + migration guide). E003 correctly carries `implementation_type: docs-authoring`. No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, consistent with no gates.\n54\t\n55\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n56\t\n57\tType-appropriate signal fields are used (ai_signals on all three Implementation epics; investigation_signals null), and all three ai-signals rationale files exist with substantive rationales. `architecture_claims: 1` on all three epics is well calibrated — each rationale cites `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, which I verified corroborates the premises (logr v1.4.3, controller-runtime v0.24.1, the 8 component controllers, the cloudmanager binary at `cmd/cloudmanager`, and the AWSKubernetesEngine/AzureKubernetesEngine/CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine CRDs).\n58\t\n59\tTwo arguable values (hence 1 point, per the rubric anchor for 1-2 arguable signals):\n60\t- E001/E002 `human_process_gates: 0` — the strategy's Prerequisites & Process Gates list seven gates all \"_Pending human review_\" (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.), which the Investigation Scan itself says \"precede all implementation work equally.\" A strict reading of the rubric condition (\"requires human approval step\") supports -1 for the implementation epics; the decomposer's 0 rationale (gates are strategy-level, not epic deliverables) is defensible but borderline.\n61\t- E003 `external_dependency: 0` — filing tracking issues in the Cluster Observability Operator projects is lightweight cross-team coordination; calling it a \"one-way notification\" to justify 0 rather than -1 is arguable, though non-gating per overlay 0008 supports it.\n62\t\n63\tE003's remaining low scores (pattern_precedent 0, existing_foundation 0, open_questions 0, human_process_gates -1) are consistent with a from-scratch guideline requiring technical review and a PM decision.\n64\t\n65\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n66\t\n67\tAll three epics have specific, testable ACs derived from the strategy's proposed ACs (audit checklist, lint-rule-measured conversions, unit tests parsing test logger output, grep for retired DSCI keys, cluster-scoped namespace behavior, before/after ingestion validation). Rule-mandated ACs: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review sign-off AC (AC 2); replacement rollback/feature-flag and konflux build-pipeline ACs are not applicable (no component replacement, no konflux chain). Soft-AC scan: the dev-cluster ingestion and SRE/Support alert-rule ACs are conditions on work the epics themselves perform and verify (documented update plan, before/after comparison), not externally-controlled preconditions — correctly placed. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n68\t\n69\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n70\t\n71\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: manager binary five tiers + 15 action files (E001), three cloudmanager controllers (E002), contribution guideline + external publication (E003). Every strategy acceptance criterion maps to an epic AC, including the cluster-scoped resource AC (E001 AC 6, E002 AC 4) and the lint-rule measurement from the first strategy AC. All four strategy risks are carried forward (missed call sites → audit checklist + lint; log consumer regression → ingestion-validation ACs; external adoption delay → E003 note; DSCI query breakage → E001 risk + E003 migration guide). All four open questions are placed (optional fields → E003 scope/AC 5; alert rules → ACs on both implementation epics; cloudmanager timing → Health Warning + E002 risk; percentage → audit ACs). The \"Design doc: to be created\" item's enumerated content (three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, DSCI migration guide) is fully covered by E003's guideline scope. Out-of-scope boundaries (non-error log paths, dashboards, external operator code changes) are respected — no COO-team epic was generated, correctly per overlay 0008. Documentation support is listed as pending (not \"Yes\"), and a docs-authoring epic exists anyway via HLR-8.\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-948328d987fec8dc","agent_id":"afbead9fffd0ac3d8","session_id":"f64427cd-cf3e-4bee-9716-c63ae53a7129","uuid":"376b1608-9b8a-4285-baf6-12640fc9f127","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:43:29.712Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d56e4f553892ba9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decompos