{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae9118731369d5c0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n10\t\n11\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n12\t\n13\t## Triage Outcome\n14\t\n15\tStep 0 was evaluated first. The strategy is S-sized (1–2 sprints), affects a single\n16\tcomponent (rhods-operator), and a single team performs all code work (ODH Operator\n17\tteam), with highly mechanical, pattern-following conversion work. However, the\n18\t**multiple distinct work streams** escape condition fires: the scope spans (a) code\n19\timplementation in the manager binary, (b) code implementation in the architecturally\n20\tdistinct cloudmanager binary (separate binary, separate controllers, deployed on\n21\tnon-OpenShift Kubernetes: AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), and (c) substantive\n22\tdocumentation authoring — a new contribution guideline specifying the convention and\n23\tits publication as a specification for external operator adoption (P1 HLR). This is\n24\tnew content authoring, not an incidental documentation touch-up. Below-threshold does\n25\tnot apply; the full decomposition (Steps 1–8) was executed.\n26\t\n27\t## Staff Engineer Input\n28\t\n29\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section was parsed first and takes precedence. The\n30\tstrategy body already incorporates the SME corrections, so no divergence remained to\n31\toverride: the corrected controller architecture (5 tiers: DSC, 8 component\n32\tcontrollers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI, 5 service controllers),\n33\tthe exact action-file breakdown (15 total: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3\n34\tmodule), the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard\n35\tkeys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`), the camelCase field\n36\tnaming convention, and cloudmanager's use of the shared reconciler (already gets\n37\t`name`/`namespace`). The wrong-premise open question about \"~16 component action\n38\tfiles\" is answered (8/8 components have exactly one) and was not decomposed.\n39\t\n40\t## Component Mapping\n41\t\n42\t- The strategy names one affected component: **rhods-operator** (manager +\n43\t  cloudmanager binaries). This name is not verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`;\n44\t  it is mapped to the canonical component **`OpenShift AI Productization`** — the\n45\t  RHOAI platform operator maintained by the ODH Operator team in the downstream\n46\t  `red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator` repository. All three epics use this single\n47\t  canonical component. Logged here as a non-obvious mapping.\n48\t- External operators (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator, owned by the Cluster\n49\t  Observability Operator team) are **not** components of this decomposition: per\n50\t  platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external\n51\t  operator dependencies. The strategy only publishes the convention for voluntary\n52\t  adoption, which is a deliverable of the documentation epic. No COO-team epic is\n53\t  generated and no passive-component validation epic is needed (nothing in RHOAI\n54\t  validates their behavior).\n55\t\n56\t## Investigation Scan\n57\t\n58\tAll unknowns were collected systematically (open questions table, risks, assumptions,\n59\tpending reviews) and each evaluated against the decision rule — *does the answer\n60\tchange which downstream epics exist or what they do?*\n61\t\n62\t| Unknown | Source | Structural? | Handling |\n63\t|---------|--------|-------------|----------|\n64\t| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., attempt count, error category)? | Open question (PM / Observability) | No — the required field set is fixed by the HLRs; extra fields would be an additive follow-up on the same call sites, not different epics | Open question recorded on the documentation epic; the guideline records the decision |\n65\t| Which existing log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns that will change? | Open question (SRE / Support) + risk | No — identifying them *is* the log-consumer regression validation step; work proceeds the same way, failure = fix-and-retry | Acceptance criterion + risk on both implementation epics |\n66\t| Should cloudmanager ship in the same release as manager, or a subsequent one? | Open question (PM / ODH Operator) | No — a scheduling decision; the binary boundary already makes the work independently schedulable, and no DAG edge exists between the binaries | Health warning; either scheduling is supported without restructuring |\n67\t| What is the actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity (SME says 75%)? | Open question + assumption | No — the audit is the first work item of the conversion epics; the method (grep/static analysis) is fixed and the answer changes volume, not epic structure or approach | Acceptance criterion (audit checklist) on both implementation epics |\n68\t| Strategy prerequisites (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.) pending human review | Prerequisites & Process Gates | No — strategy-level process gates preceding all implementation work equally | Not decomposed into epics; reflected in the `human_process_gates` signal on each epic |\n69\t\n70\t**No Investigation epics.** No conditional branches or phased decomposition are\n71\tneeded.\n72\t\n73\t## Epic List\n74\t\n75\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n76\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n77\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n78\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n79\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n80\t\n81\tComponent for all three epics: `OpenShift AI Productization` (see Component Mapping).\n82\t\n83\t## Dependency DAG\n84\t\n85\t```mermaid\n86\tgraph TD\n87\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E001<br/>manager binary log conversion (P0)\"] --> E003\n88\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E002<br/>cloudmanager binary log conversion (P0)\"] --> E003\n89\t    E003[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E003<br/>convention documentation (P1)\"]\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\tE001 and E002 run in parallel (no edge between them).\n93\t\n94\t## DAG Justification\n95\t\n96\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n97\t|------|------|-----------|\n98\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` epic is blocked by every Implementation epic: the guideline documents what was built (before/after examples, DSCI migration guide) and must be technically reviewed against the implemented conversions |\n99\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same — the published convention covers both binaries |\n100\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repository, different areas: the manager binary (OpenShift) and the cloudmanager binary (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave) share no artifacts — the field convention is a shared *standard*, not a build-against artifact. Potential merge conflicts are coordination risk, not a dependency |\n101\t\n102\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n103\t\n104\tHLRs are numbered in strategy order (HLR-1 … HLR-8).\n105\t\n106\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n107\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n108\t| HLR-1 — embedded resource identity converted to structured `name`/`namespace` in all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs | P0 | E001 (manager binary), E002 (cloudmanager) | Surface split by binary |\n109\t| HLR-2 — DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | E001 | |\n110\t| HLR-3 — service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n111\t| HLR-4 — all 15 action file error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` across 4 tiers | P0 | E001 | |\n112\t| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n113\t| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n114\t| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (cross-controller queryability) | P1 | E001 **(bundled)** | Bundled: the DSC and component-controller `resourceKind` addition lands on the *same `log.Error` call sites* E001 already restructures for HLR-1 in the same files. A separate P1 epic would force a second pass over every converted call site and duplicate the audit checklist and lint wiring — not separately schedulable. E001 is P0 regardless via its P0 HLRs |\n115\t| HLR-8 — convention documented as contribution guideline and published for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | |\n116\t\n117\tAll eight HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n118\t\n119\t**Priority derivation:** E001 and E002 are P0 by their P0 HLRs. E003 is P1 via the\n120\t`docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1),\n121\tmatching HLR-8's P1; its edges do not push priority onto the implementations.\n122\t\n123\t**Enforcement deliverable placement:** the CI lint/static-analysis rule the strategy\n124\trequires is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable. It is *not* a standalone epic —\n125\tit is divided per surface: the manager-binary conversion epic owns the lint check for\n126\tmanager binary sources, and the cloudmanager conversion epic owns the mirror item for\n127\tcloudmanager sources (each wired into CI for its own binary).\n128\t\n129\t## Health Warnings\n130\t\n131\t- **Epic kept above the ~2-week guideline (E001).** Effort estimate ~1.25–1.5 sprints\n132\t  (audit ~0.5 + conversion ~0.75–1, per the strategy's estimate). Kept as one epic\n133\t  because it is a single logical sweep over one binary's reconciliation error-log\n134\t  surface: the audit checklist, field convention, unit-test pattern, and CI lint\n135\t  wiring are shared across all five controller tiers, and a per-tier split would\n136\t  duplicate that scaffolding and fragment one convention change across artificial\n137\t  boundaries. **Conditional re-split trigger:** re-split by controller tier (e.g.,\n138\t  component controllers + action files / module controller / service controllers /\n139\t  DSC + DSCI) if the completed call-site audit finds materially more\n140\t  embedded-identity sites than the ~75% estimate implies (roughly more than double\n141\t  the expected site count), or if a single release train cannot absorb the review\n142\t  load.\n143\t- **Cloudmanager release timing (open question, PM-owned).** Whether cloudmanager\n144\t  lands in the same release as the manager binary is a scheduling decision, not a\n145\t  structural one; the decomposition keeps the two binaries independent (no DAG edge),\n146\t  so either scheduling is supported without restructuring.\n147\t- **Log consumer regression risk.** Message strings change and DSCI key names migrate;\n148\t  existing text-based alert rules (Loki, CloudWatch) may break. Captured as\n149\t  acceptance criteria and risks on both implementation epics (SRE/Support\n150\t  coordination, dev-cluster ingestion validation before merge). The single-release\n151\t  DSCI transition and before/after query examples are documented in the docs epic.\n152\t- **Strategy ACs are marked \"requires PM/Engineering validation.\"** Epic acceptance\n153\t  criteria were derived from the proposed ACs as written; if PM/Engineering validation\n154\t  changes them, the epic ACs should be revisited.\n155\t- No